{{also|płod|płód}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ɑːd|lang=en}}
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/plɒd/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-plod.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ɒd|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm|en}} *{{m|enm||plodden}} (found only in derivative {{m|enm|plodder}}), probably originally a splash through water and mud, from {{m|enm|plod||a puddle}}. Compare {{cog|nl|plodden}}, {{cog|nl|plodderen}} and {{cog|da|pladder||mire}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# A [[slow]] or [[labored]] [[walk]] or other [[motion]] or [[activity]].
#: ''We started at a brisk walk and ended at a '''plod'''.''

====Verb====
{{en-verb|plodd}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[walk]] or [[move]] [[slowly]] and [[heavily]] or [[laboriously]] (+ on, through, over).
#* '''1883''', {{w|Robert Louis Stevenson}}, ''{{w|Treasure Island}}'' Part One, Chapter 1
#** I remember him as if it were yesterday, as he came '''plodding''' to the inn door, his sea chest following behind him in a handbarrow;
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[trudge]] over or through.
# To toil; to drudge; especially, to study laboriously and patiently.
#* Drayton
#*: '''plodding''' schoolmen

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|plodder}}
* {{l|en|plodding}}

* {{seeCites}}

